<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stone Age</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Stone Age was a broad prehistoric period during which stone was widely used to make tools with an edge, a point or a percussion surface. It lasted for roughly 3.4 million years, over 99% of human history, and ended between 4000 BCE and 2000 BCE with the melting and smelting of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>; in Western Asia bronze became widespread by about 3000 BCE. It is the first period of the three-age system, followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, and is commonly divided into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, when people began to leave hunting and gathering for settled life in towns and villages as agriculture spread.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Settlements and rock carvings from about 10,000 BCE in Lapland and Finnmark, once called the Komsa culture, have not been shown to be Sámi, and older theories linking <b>Stone Age</b> cultures to them have been dismissed.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sami">Sámi · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Outsiders have often romanticised San belief: Laurens van der Post called the Bleek collections “a sort of <b>Stone Age</b> Bible”, and his critics said that his books cast the San as “children of Nature” or “mystical ecologists”.</blockquote><a href="/culture/san-people">San people · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
